	<item>
		<title>A partial digestion of the HRT counterexample</title>
		<link>https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/08/06/a-partial-digestion-of-the-hrt-counterexample/</link>
					<comments>https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/08/06/a-partial-digestion-of-the-hrt-counterexample/#comments</comments>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Terence Tao]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 06 Aug 2026 18:26:54 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[expository]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[math.CA]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[HRT conjecture]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[time-frequency analysis]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://terrytao.wordpress.com/?p=18017</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[A function of one variable can be translated in space by a spatial shift to obtain a new function and also modulated in frequency by a frequency shift to obtain a new function One can compose these two operations to obtain a time-frequency shift: (This can be viewed of as a portion of the Weyl [&#8230;]]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<p>
 A function <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%28t%29+%5Cin+L%5E2%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%28t%29+%5Cin+L%5E2%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%28t%29+%5Cin+L%5E2%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f(t) &#92;in L^2({&#92;bf R})}" class="latex" /> of one variable can be translated in space by a spatial shift <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bx%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bx%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bx%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{x}" class="latex" /> to obtain a new function </p><p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28x%2C0%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+f%28t-x%29%2C&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28x%2C0%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+f%28t-x%29%2C&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28x%2C0%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+f%28t-x%29%2C&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  &#92;pi(x,0) f(t) := f(t-x)," class="latex" /></p>
 and also modulated in frequency by a frequency shift <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Comega%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Comega%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Comega%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;omega}" class="latex" /> to obtain a new function <p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%280%2C%5Comega%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+e%5E%7B2%5Cpi+i+%5Comega+t%7D+f%28t%29.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%280%2C%5Comega%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+e%5E%7B2%5Cpi+i+%5Comega+t%7D+f%28t%29.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%280%2C%5Comega%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+e%5E%7B2%5Cpi+i+%5Comega+t%7D+f%28t%29.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  &#92;pi(0,&#92;omega) f(t) := e^{2&#92;pi i &#92;omega t} f(t)." class="latex" /></p>
 One can compose these two operations to obtain a time-frequency shift: <p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28x%2C%5Comega%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+e%5E%7B2%5Cpi+i+%5Comega+t%7D+f%28t-x%29.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28x%2C%5Comega%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+e%5E%7B2%5Cpi+i+%5Comega+t%7D+f%28t-x%29.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28x%2C%5Comega%29+f%28t%29+%3A%3D+e%5E%7B2%5Cpi+i+%5Comega+t%7D+f%28t-x%29.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  &#92;pi(x,&#92;omega) f(t) := e^{2&#92;pi i &#92;omega t} f(t-x)." class="latex" /></p>
 (This can be viewed of as a portion of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heisenberg_group">Weyl representation of the Heisenberg group</a>, but we will not adopt a representation-theoretic perspective here.)
<p>
Some functions obey finite linear relations between their time-frequency shifts. For instance, a sinusoid <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%28t%29+%3D+A+%5Csin%28k+t+%2B+%5Cphi%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%28t%29+%3D+A+%5Csin%28k+t+%2B+%5Cphi%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%28t%29+%3D+A+%5Csin%28k+t+%2B+%5Cphi%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f(t) = A &#92;sin(k t + &#92;phi)}" class="latex" /> obeys the relation </p><p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpi%7D%7Bk%7D%2C0%29+f+%2B+f+%3D+0.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpi%7D%7Bk%7D%2C0%29+f+%2B+f+%3D+0.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Cpi%28%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpi%7D%7Bk%7D%2C0%29+f+%2B+f+%3D+0.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  &#92;pi(&#92;frac{&#92;pi}{k},0) f + f = 0." class="latex" /></p>
 However, the Heil-Ramanathan-Topiwala (HRT) conjecture states that once one imposes some reasonable decay condition on <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f}" class="latex" />, no such relations exist:
<p>

</p><blockquote><b>Conjecture 1 (HRT conjecture)</b>  If <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf+%5Cin+L%5E2%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf+%5Cin+L%5E2%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf+%5Cin+L%5E2%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f &#92;in L^2({&#92;bf R})}" class="latex" /> is non-zero, then there is no relation of the form <p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+f+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_n+%5Cpi%28z_n%29+f+%3D+0&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+f+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_n+%5Cpi%28z_n%29+f+%3D+0&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+f+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_n+%5Cpi%28z_n%29+f+%3D+0&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  c_1 &#92;pi(z_1) f + &#92;dots + c_n &#92;pi(z_n) f = 0" class="latex" /></p>
 for some distinct points <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_n+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_n+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_n+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_1,&#92;dots,z_n &#92;in {&#92;bf R}^2}" class="latex" /> and some coefficients <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_n+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_n+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_n+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{c_1,&#92;dots,c_n &#92;in {&#92;bf C}}" class="latex" />, not all zero. </blockquote>

<p>


</p><p>
A special case of the HRT conjecture, which was also open, makes the additional assumption that <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f}" class="latex" /> was Schwartz.
</p><p>
Many positive results towards this conjecture were known. I will mention only a few here. There is a <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9807057">result of Linnell</a> that the conjecture is true if <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_n%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_n%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_n%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_1,&#92;dots,z_n}" class="latex" /> lie in a translate of a discrete subgroup of <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf R}^2}" class="latex" />; this (together with an argument handling the collinear case) establishes all cases where <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn+%5Cleq+3%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn+%5Cleq+3%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn+%5Cleq+3%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{n &#92;leq 3}" class="latex" />, and several partial results involving the <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn%3D4%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn%3D4%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn%3D4%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{n=4}" class="latex" /> cases are also known. The conjecture is also known if <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f}" class="latex" /> is decays at a suitably super-exponential rate, by <a href="//math.umd.edu/~rvbalan/TEACHING/RIT2023/Bownick_Speegel2012.pdf">work of Bownik and Speegle</a>.
</p><p>
I was aware of this conjecture through various talks and conversations with colleagues, and even briefly tried my hand at it for a while, though not with particularly serious effort (or progress). It was thus a nice surprise to see that it has <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05044">just been resolved by Faulhuber, Petersen, van Velthoven, and Voigtlaender</a>, even in the Schwartz case:
</p><p>

</p><blockquote><b>Theorem 2</b>  There exist complex numbers <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B12%7D%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B12%7D%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B12%7D%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{c_1,&#92;dots,c_{12}&#92;in {&#92;bf C}}" class="latex" />, not all zero, distinct points <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B12%7D+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B12%7D+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B12%7D+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_1,&#92;dots,z_{12} &#92;in {&#92;bf R}^2}" class="latex" />, and a non-zero Schwartz function <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf_%2A+%5Cin+%5Cmathcal%7BS%7D%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf_%2A+%5Cin+%5Cmathcal%7BS%7D%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf_%2A+%5Cin+%5Cmathcal%7BS%7D%28%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f_* &#92;in &#92;mathcal{S}({&#92;bf R})}" class="latex" /> such that <p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+f_%2A+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_%7B12%7D+%5Cpi%28z_%7B12%7D%29+f_%2A+%3D+0.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+f_%2A+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_%7B12%7D+%5Cpi%28z_%7B12%7D%29+f_%2A+%3D+0.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+f_%2A+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_%7B12%7D+%5Cpi%28z_%7B12%7D%29+f_%2A+%3D+0.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  c_1 &#92;pi(z_1) f_* + &#92;dots + c_{12} &#92;pi(z_{12}) f_* = 0." class="latex" /></p>
 </blockquote>

<p>


</p><p>
It is perhaps unsurprising in this current era that this result is AI-assisted. However, I think the authors have disclosed their AI use responsibly, with the final arguments written by hand with a readable overview of the argument, as well as proper discussion of methods, relation to past literature, and other independent numerical checks on the result.
</p><p>
The negative result lies only a little beyond the positive results: <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bn%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{n}" class="latex" /> is now increased to <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B12%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B12%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B12%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{12}" class="latex" />, and all but one of the points <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B12%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B12%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B12%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_1,&#92;dots,z_{12}}" class="latex" /> lie in (a translate of) a discrete subgroup of <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf R}^2}" class="latex" /> (in fact the explicit subgroup <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf Z} &#92;times &#92;frac{1}{2}{&#92;bf Z}}" class="latex" /> is used). The functions constructed are smooth and rapidly decaying, but not analytic or super-exponentially decaying, which would start being in conflict with the known positive results. 
</p><p>
In addition to AI being used to come up with the initial proof strategy, a more traditional numerical computation was used to verify one step of the argument.
</p><p>
I have not had the time to do a full digestion of the result, but (after reading the introduction, and <a href="https://chatgpt.com/share/6a74dfc3-c614-83e8-b36b-9b07bf8d55fa">using a little AI assistance of my own</a>) I was able to understand the main ideas at a high level. The first few reductions are relatively standard. Setting <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_%7B12%7D+%3D+0%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_%7B12%7D+%3D+0%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_%7B12%7D+%3D+0%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_{12} = 0}" class="latex" /> and <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_%7B12%7D+%3D+-c_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_%7B12%7D+%3D+-c_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_%7B12%7D+%3D+-c_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{c_{12} = -c_*}" class="latex" />, one can view the problem as one of solving an eigenvalue problem </p><p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%28c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_%7B11%7D+%5Cpi%28z_%7B11%7D%29%29+f_%2A+%3D+c_%2A+f_%2A.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%28c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_%7B11%7D+%5Cpi%28z_%7B11%7D%29%29+f_%2A+%3D+c_%2A+f_%2A.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%28c_1+%5Cpi%28z_1%29+%2B+%5Cdots+%2B+c_%7B11%7D+%5Cpi%28z_%7B11%7D%29%29+f_%2A+%3D+c_%2A+f_%2A.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  (c_1 &#92;pi(z_1) + &#92;dots + c_{11} &#92;pi(z_{11})) f_* = c_* f_*." class="latex" /></p>
 The time-frequency shifts <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_1,&#92;dots,z_{11}}" class="latex" /> are chosen to lie in a translate of the discrete subgroup <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf Z} &#92;times &#92;frac{1}{2}{&#92;bf Z}}" class="latex" /> by a certain irrational shift <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%28%5Calpha%2C+%5Cbeta%2F2%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%28%5Calpha%2C+%5Cbeta%2F2%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%28%5Calpha%2C+%5Cbeta%2F2%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{(&#92;alpha, &#92;beta/2)}" class="latex" />. If one was working in the shifts of a standard lattice <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf Z} &#92;times {&#92;bf Z}}" class="latex" />, it would be natural to work with the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zak_transform">Zak transform</a> of <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bf_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{f_*}" class="latex" />, but it turns out that the approach does not quite work when doing this for topological reasons (relating to the fact that scalar quasiperiodic functions of mean zero are forced to have zeroes), and so the authors used the slightly denser lattice instead <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D+%5Ctimes+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7B%5Cbf+Z%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf Z} &#92;times &#92;frac{1}{2}{&#92;bf Z}}" class="latex" />, which relates to a vector-valued version of the Zak transform taking values in <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf C}^2}" class="latex" /> rather than <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{{&#92;bf C}}" class="latex" />. Applying this transform, the eigenvalue problem can be transformed by standard calculations to a &#8220;vector cocycle problem&#8221; <a name="bzt"><p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_%2A%28z%29+F_%2A%28z+-+%5Ctau%29+%3D+c_%2A+F_%2A%28z%29%2C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%281%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_%2A%28z%29+F_%2A%28z+-+%5Ctau%29+%3D+c_%2A+F_%2A%28z%29%2C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%281%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_%2A%28z%29+F_%2A%28z+-+%5Ctau%29+%3D+c_%2A+F_%2A%28z%29%2C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%281%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  B_*(z) F_*(z - &#92;tau) = c_* F_*(z), &#92; &#92; &#92; &#92; &#92; (1)" class="latex" /></p>
</a> where <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{F_* : {&#92;bf R}^2 &#92;rightarrow {&#92;bf C}^2}" class="latex" /> is a non-zero smooth quasiperiodic vector-valued function, <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;tau}" class="latex" /> is an irrational shift <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%3D+%28%5Calpha%2C%5Cbeta%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%3D+%28%5Calpha%2C%5Cbeta%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%3D+%28%5Calpha%2C%5Cbeta%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;tau= (&#92;alpha,&#92;beta)}" class="latex" />, and <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{B_*(z)}" class="latex" /> is a certain explicit <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B2+%5Ctimes+2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B2+%5Ctimes+2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B2+%5Ctimes+2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{2 &#92;times 2}" class="latex" /> matrix-valued function depending on the choices of <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{c_1,&#92;dots,c_{11}}" class="latex" />, <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_1,&#92;dots,z_{11}}" class="latex" />, and <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;tau}" class="latex" />.
<p>
How to solve this equation? The motivating scenario here is if the matrix function <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{B_*(z)}" class="latex" /> was replaced by a rank one function </p><p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_0%28z%29+%3D+%5Cchi%28z%29+%5Cchi%28z-%5Ctau%29%5E%2A&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_0%28z%29+%3D+%5Cchi%28z%29+%5Cchi%28z-%5Ctau%29%5E%2A&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_0%28z%29+%3D+%5Cchi%28z%29+%5Cchi%28z-%5Ctau%29%5E%2A&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  B_0(z) = &#92;chi(z) &#92;chi(z-&#92;tau)^*" class="latex" /></p>
 for some smooth vector-valued function <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Cchi+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Cchi+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Cchi+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;chi : {&#92;bf R}^2 &#92;rightarrow {&#92;bf C}}" class="latex" /> of unit magnitude. Then one could solve the equation by taking <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A%28z%29+%3D+%5Cchi%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A%28z%29+%3D+%5Cchi%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A%28z%29+%3D+%5Cchi%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{F_*(z) = &#92;chi(z)}" class="latex" /> and <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_%2A+%3D+1%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_%2A+%3D+1%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_%2A+%3D+1%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{c_* = 1}" class="latex" />. It is not possible to make the function <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{B_*}" class="latex" /> exactly of this form, but through some numerical computation and clever AI-assisted guesswork, the authors were able to find a choice of <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bc_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cc_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{c_1,&#92;dots,c_{11}}" class="latex" /> and <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz_1%2C%5Cdots%2Cz_%7B11%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z_1,&#92;dots,z_{11}}" class="latex" />, and <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;tau}" class="latex" /> that made <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_%2A%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{B_*}" class="latex" /> <em>approximately equal</em> to a rank one function <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_0%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_0%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BB_0%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{B_0}" class="latex" /> of this form, in fact getting a uniform estimate <p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Csup_%7Bz+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D+%5C%7C+B_%2A%28z%29+-+B_0%28z%29+%5C%7C_%7Bop%7D+%3C+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B3%7D.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Csup_%7Bz+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D+%5C%7C+B_%2A%28z%29+-+B_0%28z%29+%5C%7C_%7Bop%7D+%3C+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B3%7D.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Csup_%7Bz+%5Cin+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2%7D+%5C%7C+B_%2A%28z%29+-+B_0%28z%29+%5C%7C_%7Bop%7D+%3C+%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B3%7D.&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  &#92;sup_{z &#92;in {&#92;bf R}^2} &#92;| B_*(z) - B_0(z) &#92;|_{op} &lt; &#92;frac{1}{3}." class="latex" /></p>
 As it turns out, such an approximation is sufficient to run a contraction mapping argument to find a solution to a variant of <a href="#bzt">(1)</a>, namely <p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_%2A%28z%29+v_%2A%28z+-+%5Ctau%29+%3D+q_%2A%28z%29+v_%2A%28z%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_%2A%28z%29+v_%2A%28z+-+%5Ctau%29+%3D+q_%2A%28z%29+v_%2A%28z%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++B_%2A%28z%29+v_%2A%28z+-+%5Ctau%29+%3D+q_%2A%28z%29+v_%2A%28z%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  B_*(z) v_*(z - &#92;tau) = q_*(z) v_*(z)" class="latex" /></p>
 for some smooth <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bv_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bv_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bv_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%5E2%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{v_* : {&#92;bf R}^2 &#92;rightarrow {&#92;bf C}^2}" class="latex" /> and <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bq_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bq_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bq_%2A+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{q_* : {&#92;bf R}^2 &#92;rightarrow {&#92;bf C}}" class="latex" />. (Here it was important to get the operator norm bound below <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B3%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B3%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B3%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;frac{1}{3}}" class="latex" />; they are barely able to do this, with a numerically obtained bound of <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B0.333032%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B0.333032%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B0.333032%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{0.333032}" class="latex" />, though this bound might not be optimal.)
<p>
The main remaining obstacle is that the &#8220;eigenvalue function&#8221; <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bq_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bq_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bq_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{q_*(z)}" class="latex" /> is varying in the parameter <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bz%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{z}" class="latex" /> rather than constant. (This issue was, by the way, anticipated to some extent in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.0732">previous work of Demeter</a>, who observed that eigenfunctions of the almost Matthieu discrete Schrödinger operator gave a near-miss counterexample to the HRT conjecture, but with an eigenvalue that depended on an auxiliary phase shift parameter rather than constant.) However, if one was able to solve the scalar cocycle equation <a name="qzh"></a></p><p align="center"><a name="qzh"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++q_%2A%28z%29+h%28z-%5Ctau%29+%3D+c_%2A+h%28z%29+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%282%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++q_%2A%28z%29+h%28z-%5Ctau%29+%3D+c_%2A+h%28z%29+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%282%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++q_%2A%28z%29+h%28z-%5Ctau%29+%3D+c_%2A+h%28z%29+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%282%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  q_*(z) h(z-&#92;tau) = c_* h(z) &#92; &#92; &#92; &#92; &#92; (2)" class="latex" /></a></p><a name="qzh">
</a> for some smooth <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bh+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bh+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7Bh+%3A+%7B%5Cbf+R%7D%5E2+%5Crightarrow+%7B%5Cbf+C%7D%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{h : {&#92;bf R}^2 &#92;rightarrow {&#92;bf C}}" class="latex" />, then one could solve the equation <a href="#bzt">(1)</a> by setting <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A%28z%29+%3D+h%28z%29+v_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A%28z%29+%3D+h%28z%29+v_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7BF_%2A%28z%29+%3D+h%28z%29+v_%2A%28z%29%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{F_*(z) = h(z) v_*(z)}" class="latex" />. The approach to solve <a href="#qzh">(2)</a> is standard: take logarithms, apply a Fourier transform, and then divide out by the multiplier associated to the <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;tau}" class="latex" /> shift. This can cause a well-known &#8220;small divisor&#8221; problem (which arises in various dynamical contexts, such as in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolmogorov%E2%80%93Arnold%E2%80%93Moser_theorem">KAM theorem</a>) if <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;tau}" class="latex" /> behaves too much like a rational vector, but the standard resolution to this is to select a shift <img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%7B%5Ctau%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="{&#92;tau}" class="latex" /> that obeys good Diophantine approximation properties. For the purposes of numerics the authors selected an extremely concrete shift, namely <p align="center"><img src="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Ctau+%3D+%282%5E%7B1%2F3%7D+-+1%2C+2%5E%7B2%2F3%7D+-+1%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002" srcset="https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Ctau+%3D+%282%5E%7B1%2F3%7D+-+1%2C+2%5E%7B2%2F3%7D+-+1%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002 1x, https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=%5Cdisplaystyle++%5Ctau+%3D+%282%5E%7B1%2F3%7D+-+1%2C+2%5E%7B2%2F3%7D+-+1%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002&#038;zoom=4.5 4x" alt="&#92;displaystyle  &#92;tau = (2^{1/3} - 1, 2^{2/3} - 1)" class="latex" /></p>
 but I get the impression that the exact choice here was not crucial for the argument, and that many other irrational algebraic numbers could have worked here.
<p></p>
